    <!-- Use paragraphs to break apart subsections -->
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Course Description</title>

      <p>
        Physics 193 is the first part of a two-semester calculus-based
        introduction to physics, with a co-requisite of Calculus II (Math 132 or
        its equivalent).
        The course is an advanced first-year course in classical mechanics,
        taught at a more sophisticated level than Physics 191.
        The approach is that of an upper-division physics course, with more
        emphasis on the underlying formal structure, rather than breadth of
        topics.
        The main goal is to provide an in-depth c overage of the physical laws
        that govern the motion of objects, forces, and forms of energy in
        mechanical systems, as well as an introduction to special relativity.
        The course is particularly addressed to students considering a physics
        or mathematics heavy science/engineering majors.
        L31 193 students should enroll in a L31 1741 laboratory section.
        Students may not receive credit for more than one of Physics 117A, 197,
        191 and 193.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-learning-obj">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Apply Newton's Laws and conservation principles to describe the
            motion of point-like and rigid bodies
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Systematically approach problems using a combination of conceptual
            and mathematical arguments
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret the mathematical solutions of mechanics problems in
            physical terms
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Improve your physical intuition by analyzing difficult problems and
            unexpected situations
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Analyze physical arguments and determine their logical correctness
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Practice structuring your physical intuition into a solid logical
            argument
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Collaborate with other students to solve challenging problems
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
  <subsection xml:id="sec-assessments">
    <title>Assessments and Grades</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-grading-phil">
      <title>Grading Philosophy</title>

      <p>
        Learning requires deliberate practice and engagement in a feedback loop.
        Deliberate practice means practice with intention, attempting to improve
        your mastery over specific skills or ideas in a way that is repeatable
        and meaningful, as opposed to
        <q>I'll read the textbook 10 times and
        eventually it'll make sense</q>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A feedback loop means trying something, receiving feedback, and using
        the feedback to practice so you can try again.
        Long story short, it means working hard and thinking hard.
        The assignment structure for the course has practice and feedback loops
        built in so you can try things, struggle productively, and learn from
        your work.
        We also firmly believe that you are able to master all the fundamental
        skills in the class, and so reattempts are built into every course
        assignment.
      </p>

      <p>
        The grade mapping for the course will use <q>checkmarks</q> or
        <q>achievements</q> instead of traditional points (see grading chart
        below).
        This should be easier for you to track, but more importantly, your grade
        is tied to specific skills, so the answer to the question
        <q>how do I
        improve my grade?</q> will always be:
        <q>practice something until you know
        how to do it better</q>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since there are no points, all of the grading is binary: <q>yes</q> or
        <q>no</q>.
        That said, we are <em>not expecting perfection</em>.
        It certainly makes sense to strive to do things perfectly (whatever that
        means), but we understand that even perfect understanding does not
        necessarily translate to perfect performance.
        See the <q>Expectations on Assignments</q> document for more details on
        this.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-Detailed">
      <title>Detailed Assignment Descriptions</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <title>Engagement Credits</title>

            <p>
              Taking charge of your own learning and engaging with the content
              and skills in a college course is a key component of success.
              This means preparing for class, attending class and participating
              in in-class activities, doing deliberate practice (homework,
              office hours, reflection), and more.
            </p>

            <p>
              Cultivating these habits is an important academic skill, and so
              activities that require mindful engagement with the course will be
              awarded engagement credits.
              Successfully completing these assignments will award you credits
              that will be tallied on Canvas.
              These activities are:
            </p>

            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Class preparation: a good-faith effort in completing reading
                    quizzes will award you 2 engagement credits.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Class attendance: coming to class and staying for most of
                    the class session will award you 1 engagement credit.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Student hour attendance: coming to any class help session
                    will award you 1 engagement credit.
                  </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                  <p>
                    Self-reflection: submitting a reflection assignment (open
                    every week on Canvas) will award you 2 engagement credits.
                    Think of these as a make-up for student hours.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Homework</title>

            <p>
              Every week on Monday, a homework assignment will be released
              covering the content of the week.
              Problems will have a range of difficulties so you can reflect on
              your work and the main lessons of each question before moving onto
              the next one.
            </p>

            <p>
              The goal of the homework problems is not for us to test your
              understanding of the material, but for you to test yourself and
              have a first chance at practicing the week's material.
              As such, homework will be graded for completion, but you will
              still receive detailed feedback on each problem (see the
              <q>Expectations on Assignments</q> document).
            </p>

            <p>
              Note: if your problem set is significantly incomplete or
              containing significant physics mistakes, we will ask you to attend
              office hours/book a meeting with one of the course instructors to
              discuss the problems before earning credit for that week.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Fundamental Skills</title>

            <p>
              Mechanics is a foundational subject in your study of physics.
              Regardless of where your studies ultimately take you, you will
              engage with the concepts and techniques in this course repeatedly.
              This means that there are several skills that need to be mastered
              before moving forward.
            </p>

            <p>
              We have called these <q>fundamental skills</q>, and you can about
              them in the <q>Fundamental Skills</q> document on Canvas.
              The 15 fundamental skills will be evaluated in 4 exams.
              Each skill will be tested in a problem (possibly with a few parts
              and follow-ups), and each problem will be graded as
              <q>Successful</q> or <q>Retry</q>.
            </p>

            <p>
              There is no partial credit but note the possible grade of
              <q>Retry</q>.
              If you are not successful initially, you will have time to study
              and try your hand at the skill again.
              Since exams include retries, they will be quite long, and we do
              not expect you to complete an entire exam if you need to retry a
              few learning targets.
              Keep this in mind when you decide which problems to do for each
              exam.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <title>Challenge Problems</title>

            <p>
              Physics (every science, in fact) is more than a collection of
              facts, formulas, and experimental procedures.
              It is a way of looking at the world and analyzing problems using a
              conceptual and quantitative framework.
              Challenge problems are an opportunity for you to think deeply
              about how the different topics and techniques you will encounter
              in the class are interconnected.
            </p>

            <p>
              There will be 5 challenge problems during the semester, which must
              be completed in pairs.
              Each challenge problem consists of two steps: a first submission,
              which will be returned by the grader with feedback, and a
              revision.
              Challenge problems are graded as <q>Successful</q> or
              <q>Needs Revision</q> (see the expectations page on Canvas for
              more information).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-grades">
      <title>Determining Course Grades</title>

      <p>
        Unlike traditional grading schemes, your grade is determined by your
        success in the learning targets, challenge problems, and homework
        completion separately.
        This means that you cannot use your homework grade to boost your exam
        grade, for instance.
        The reason for doing this is clarity: averaging different assignments
        can make it difficult for you to determine what you are doing well and
        what needs work.
      </p>

      <p>
        The learning targets are all listed on Canvas, and you will know after
        an exam what you did well and what you need to work on.
        This means that <q>improving your grade</q> should always have a clear
        path: study the topics you had trouble and try again on the next exam.
        The challenge problems are also evaluated separately from your exams,
        since they are measuring something different, which is your ability to
        work in groups and to <q>stretch</q> your physics skills.
      </p>

      <p>
        I know that grades can be a source of stress but do remember that not
        everything you turn in needs to be perfect.
        And remember: there are second chances for every assignment!
        The table below lists the success requirements for each grade level in
        terms of learning targets, challenge problems, and homework assignments.
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>Grade modifiers:</alert> If you earned over 90 engagement credits
        and satisfied the requirements for a <q>base grade</q>, you will get a +
        grade.
        For example: 9 learning targets, 3 challenge problems, and 10 homework
        assignments, along with 100 engagement credits is a B+.
        If you have at least one learning target and one challenge problem above
        the requirement for a particular grade you will earn the next highest
        minus grade.
        Example: 10 learning targets, 4 challenge problems, and 10 homework
        assignments is an A <minus/>
      </p>

      <tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" valign="top" halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Grade</cell>
          <cell>Learning Targets</cell>
          <cell>Challenge Problems</cell>
          <cell>Homework Assignments</cell>
          <cell>Engagement Credits (+)</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>A</cell>
          <cell>13</cell>
          <cell>5</cell>
          <cell>11</cell>
          <cell>70</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>B</cell>
