          <cell>N/A</cell>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>N/A</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Additional">
    <title>Additional Recommended Textbooks</title>

    <p>
      The list below contains additional resources to help you study for this
      class.
      There is plenty more to be found online and in the library, but this is a
      curated list that will help you in this semester and beyond.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>Introduction to Classical Mechanics</pubtitle> by D.J
            Morin.
            This book is at about the same level as the main text.
            It has many problems and solutions, and approaches the material
            creatively.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>The Feynman Lectures on Physics</pubtitle> by R.P.
            Feynman, R.
            Leighton, and M.
            Sands.
            This text is now
            <url href="https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/">available online for free
            </url>.
            This isn't a textbook, but lots of students appreciate Feynman's
            colorful explanation of the material, though some of the language is
            quite dated.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>Physics, 5th Ed</pubtitle> by D.
            Halliday, R.
            Resnick, and K.
            Krane.
            This book is at a lower level than our textbook, but it's definitely
            worth having it around if you feel lost at some point and need to
            take a step back before approaching K&amp;K.
            Freely available through the library.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>Introduction to Special Relativity</pubtitle> by R.
            Resnick.
            A very clear and methodical approach to special relativity.
            Worth reading if you find the main text's tangents too distracting.
            Freely available through the Internet Archive.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>Special Relativity</pubtitle> by A.P.
            French.
            This book has a good focus on physical intuition.
            It includes experimental data and has interesting problems.
            Freely available through the library.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

