  <subsection xml:id="subsec-advice">
    <title>General Advice</title>
    <p>
      <alert>This section is also provided as a separate document
        on Canvas if you wish to have a copy of just this part.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Learning is a messy process.
      Research on cognitive science tells us that the most effective forms of
      learning are the ones that require your active engagement and effort, as
      well as those that confront misconceptions and mistakes head on.
      As you likely have experienced, struggling through something is not a
      comfortable process, but it can be very rewarding.
      I want you to think and work hard on the problems and concepts we will
      discuss so that you can construct your own understanding of the course
      topics in your mind, that is there for you when you need it in the future.
      Here's some advice from me to help you set yourself up for success.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <alert>Perfectionism:</alert> I will often tell you to
          <q>aim for flawless solutions</q>, but please don't think that
          everything you submit needs to be perfect.
          <q>Aim for flawless</q> means that once you have solved a problem, you
          should be able to review your solution and understand its meaning
          clearly.
          Every step flows logically from the assumptions you made, there are no
          guesses in your solution, the mathematics is clear, and your algebraic
          solution passes basic sanity checks.
          This is a high bar, and I want you to try and reach it because I
          believe in you.
          That said, you are also learning, and it is also my job to give you
          feedback.
          I prefer seeing a page full of questions than a blank page, or work
          that isn't yours
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Don't overcommit:</alert> I've seen first year students
            compete to see who can take the most credits and sleep the fewest
            hours.
            I recommend you use your first semester to adjust to being in
            college, learn effective study strategies
            <url href="https://ctl.wustl.edu/learningcenter/resources/six-strategies-for-effective-learning/">
            like the ones provided by the Learning Center
          </url>,
            and try out new things.
            This course is 4 credit units, and the university's general
            expectation is that one credit unit should take you 2 to 3 hours of
            work outside of class.
            I want you to have time to think about physics and to talk to your
            classmates about physics.
            Note that I'm not asking you to not be ambitious, just to try and
            know your limits.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>A personal anecdote on overcommitting:</alert> I took the
            equivalent of 25 credits in my second semester of college, and
            between classes and labs I was in a classroom for about 35h a week.
            It did not go well for me, but it did get me to focus on physics
            classes the following term.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Ask for help early and often:</alert> If you need help with
            something, let me know right away.
            I am here to help you, and I want to help you.
            If you need an exception with an assignment, or extra help, or
            something came up, or ( <ellipsis/> ), let me know as soon as
            possible.
            From a practical perspective, physics builds on itself, so keeping
            pace with the course is important, and if you wait to tell me that
            there's something wrong, it gets harder to help you.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Collaboration:</alert> I encourage you to work with other
            students, form study groups, and collaborate as much as you can.
            Challenge problems will also require collaboration: individual
            submissions are not accepted.
            Collaboration is an important part of how modern science and
            engineering are done, so it is important to practice those skills
            from the start.
            We have also observed that students who work with others have more
            fun and do better in the course.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Pitfalls of collaboration:</alert> It is easy to join a study
            group and not actively engage in discussing problems, instead just
            listening to a classmate who is more confident or talkative.
            You must make sure that everything you turn in represents your
            understanding and your thoughts.
            Otherwise, the feedback you will receive from the instructors will
            not be useful and you might not understand how, for example, your
            homework assignments translate into exam performance.
            Start the homework assignment <em>before</em> you meet with other
            students, so you have your own ideas on how to attempt the problems.
            When you are working in a group, don't let one person take over
            every discussion.
            Rotate who starts discussion of a problem.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>WRITE NEATLY!</alert> The graders are instructed not to look
            for deep meaning in random disorganized scribbles.
            Clarity of presentation is clarity of thought.
            You do not need to be verbose; but you do need to be neat and clear.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Late assignments:</alert> Challenge problems and reading
            quizzes have strict deadlines.
            I will accept late homework if you email me 24h before the homework
            assignment with a reasonable plan for when the homework will be
            completed.
            You don't have to explain why you're missing the deadline, but if
            this is happening often, I reserve the right to ask for a meeting
            before extending the deadline.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Note-taking:</alert> I encourage you to take your notes on
            paper, rather than on your computer.
            There is some evidence that this is better for your long-term
            retention and memory.
            If you want to have electronic notes, something like a tablet is
            recommended so you can write instead of type.
            I will also try to keep the classroom environment as analog as
            possible since I know screens are distracting and make it harder to
            focus.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>ChatGPT and other LLMs</alert>:With all of the above advice
            in mind, I will recommend that you not use ChatGPT or other LLMs to
            study for this class.
            In a sense, LLMs are like the ultimate version of the
            <q>very confident classmate</q>.
            Whatever input you give to them, they will generate text output that
            answers the question
            <q>What would an answer to this question sound like?</q>.
            This is not the same thing as answering the question, and it's
            important to know that LLMs make mistakes, and those mistakes can be
            subtle and ultimately set you back.
          </p>

          <p>
            More importantly for me, LLMs generate answers quickly, and as such
            they short-circuit the learning process of thinking hard and
            constructing understanding in your mind.
            You read the LLM output, tell yourself
            <q>Yeah, that makes sense</q>, and then you forget it.
            Evidence on LLM usage in education is still in its early stages, but
            the picture that seems to be emerging is that reliance on LLMs is
            detrimental to learning and can lead to dependence on these systems.
            This goes against the goal of this course of training you to think
            critically.
          </p>

          <p>
            All that said, I will not police your LLM usage beyond the basic
            academic integrity requirements, since I'm not a cop.
            Copying an assignment on an LLM and submitting the prompt counts as
            plagiarism.
            What I actually care about is if you do that, you miss an
            opportunity to get feedback and grow by reflecting on the feedback.
            If you do use an LLM, keep this in mind, as well as the effective
            study strategies mentioned earlier in this document.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-Required">
    <title>Required Policies</title>

    <p>
      The following policies are not specific to this course, but list important
      things you should know here at WashU
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <title>ACADEMIC INTEGRITY</title>

          <p>
            Effective learning, teaching and research all depend upon the
            ability of members of the academic community to trust one another
            and to trust the integrity of work that is submitted for academic
            credit or conducted in the wider arena of scholarly research.
            Such an atmosphere of mutual trust fosters the free exchange of
            ideas and enables all members of the community to achieve their
            highest potential.
          </p>

          <p>
            In all academic work, the ideas and contributions of others
            (including generative artificial intelligence) must be appropriately
            acknowledged and work that is presented as original must be, in
            fact, original.
            You should familiarize yourself with the appropriate academic
            integrity policies of your academic program(s).
          </p>

          <p>
            For more information on academic integrity, see the
            <url href="https://policies.washu.edu/policies/academic-integrity-policy-for-undergraduate-students/">
              Undergraduate Academic Integrity Policy</url>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>UNAUTHORIZED RECORDING AND DISTRIBUTION OF CLASSROOM ACTIVITIES &amp; MATERIALS</title>

          <p>
            The following applies to all students in my class:
            <q>Except as
            otherwise expressly authorized by the instructor or the university,
            students may not record, stream, reproduce, display, publish or
            further distribute any classroom activities or course materials.
            This includes lectures, class discussions, advising meetings, office
            hours, assessments, problems, answers, presentations, slides,
            screenshots or other materials presented as part of the course.
            If a student with a disability wishes to request the use of
            assistive technology as a reasonable accommodation, the student must
            first contact the Office of Disability Resources to seek approval.
            If recording is permitted, unauthorized use or distribution of
            recordings is also prohibited.</q>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>DISABILITY RESOURCES (DR)</title>

          <p>
            WashU supports the right of all enrolled students to an equitable
            educational opportunity and strives to create an inclusive learning
            environment.
            In the event the physical or online environment results in barriers
            to your inclusion due to a disability, please contact WashU's
            Disability Resources (DR) as soon as possible and engage in a
            process for determining and communicating reasonable accommodations.
            As soon as possible after receiving an accommodation from DR, send
            me your WashU Accommodation Letter.
            Remember that accommodations cannot be applied retroactively.
            Visit the
            <url href="disability.wustl.edu">DR website for more information</url>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>SEXUAL HARASSMENT AND ASSAULT</title>

          <p>
            If you are a victim of sexual discrimination, harassment or
            violence, we encourage you to speak with someone as soon as
            possible.
            Understand that if you choose to speak to me as an instructor, I
            must report your disclosure to my department chair, dean, or the
            Gender Equity and Title IX Compliance Officer, which may trigger an
            investigation into the incident.
          </p>

          <p>
            You may also reach out to the
            <url href="https://rsvpcenter.washu.edu/">Relationship &amp; Sexual Violence Prevention
              (RSVP) Center</url>
            to discuss your rights and your options with individuals who are not
            mandatory reporters.
            Visit the
            <url href="titleix.wustl.edu/students/confidentiality-resources-support/">Washu Title IX website</url>
            for more information
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>RELIGIOUS HOLIDAYS</title>

          <p>
            To ensure that accommodations may be made for students who miss
            class, assignments, or exams to observe a religious holiday, you
            must inform me in writing before the end of the third week of class,
            or as soon as possible if the holiday occurs during the first three
            weeks of the semester.
            For more information, please see the university's
            <url href="https://bulletin.wustl.edu/washu/calendar/Religious-Holidays.pdf">Religious Holiday Class
              Absence Policy.</url>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>EMERGENCY PREPAREDNESS</title>

          <p>
            Before an emergency affects our class, students can take steps to be
            prepared by downloading the
            <url href="https://emergency.washu.edu/washu-alert-system/washusafe-app/">WashU SAFE App.</url>
            In addition, each classroom contains a
            <q>Quick Guide for Emergencies</q> near the door.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>RESOURCES FOR STUDENTS</title>

          <p>
            WashU provides a wealth of support services that address academic,
            personal, and professional needs.
            To start exploring resources that can help you along the way, please
            visit:
            <url href="https://provost.washu.edu/resources/instructor-resources/student-resources/">Resources for Students</url>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Important resources to pay attention on this list are The Learning
            Center (academic support), CCPS (mental health support), the Taylor
            Family Center (support for 1st generation students), and WashU Cares
            (additional mental health support).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>


</section>